// =============================================================================
// Surfaces: where a band was measured, at whatever scale the paper said.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This file is JSONC (JSON with Comments). The build step strips // and /* */
// comments before shipping clean JSON to the browser.
//
// TWO LEVELS
// "Where was this measured" does not have one answer, it has a scale. Every
// entry carries a `level`:
//
//   material   a piece of solid that exists physically: one compound (ZnO,
//              γ-Al₂O₃, sapphire, KBr, ZSM-5) or a whole catalyst, electrode
//              or model (Cu/ZnO, CuZn/SiO₂, Ag/Au on Si). Never a bare metal:
//              a metal is only its atom, so a catalyst names Cu⁰ directly.
//   site       the atom-scale spot an adsorbate binds to, or an ion a
//              material is made of: Cu⁺, Al³⁺, O²⁻, Vₒ, a silanol, the
//              Pt⁰-Ce³⁺ perimeter.
//
// An oxide written only to express a composition is not a material of its
// own: ZSM-5 is made of Si⁴⁺, Al³⁺ and O²⁻, not Al₂O₃ + Na₂O + SiO₂, its
// counter-cation is a refinement (H-ZSM-5 adds H⁺), and the Na in
// Ru/"Na₂O"/Al₂O₃ is an Na⁺ site.
//
// ONE COMPOUND OR A COMPOSITE
// build.py derives `compound` on every material: true where it is made only
// of ions (cation, anion, defect, hydroxyl, bronsted sites), through its parts
// and the record it refines; false where a metal atom, a zero-valent
// non-metal, an interface or another material is among them. It is not
// authored, so it cannot disagree with the parts.
//
// THE CLAIM DECIDES THE ROLE
// TiO₂ is one record whether a paper measured bare titania or Pt on titania.
// Which it was, the claim says by what else it names. build.py derives
// `specimens` on every claim, the materials it names that no other material
// it names contains: ["tio2", "pt_moox_tio2"] has the catalyst as specimen and
// titania as the part the species was placed on; ["tio2"] alone has titania
// as specimen. It also derives `placement`: site (a site is named), material
// (a part is named, or the specimen is one compound) or specimen (only a
// composite is named: seen there, not located in it). See bands.jsonc.
//
// TWO LINKS, AND ONLY TWO
//   parts    "is made of". Points to records on the same level or lower,
//            never up. A material lists the materials it contains, and a
//            site directly only where that site belongs to no material it
//            lists (an isolated SOMC cation, an exchanged cation, a
//            zero-valent metal atom, an interface). A compound lists its
//            ions: Al₂O₃ is Al³⁺ + O²⁻, KBr is K⁺ + Br⁻. A composite site
//            lists the simpler sites it is built from. Elements flow UP
//            along it.
//   refines  "is a more specific version of". Points to exactly one record on
//            the same level. Searches flow DOWN along it: asking for Al₂O₃
//            also finds γ-Al₂O₃, asking for γ-Al₂O₃ finds only γ. A refinement
//            inherits its parent's parts and elements, may add parts of its
//            own (ZnO₁₋ₓ adds the vacancies), and must say what it adds with
//            at least one of the refinement attributes below.
//
// ELEMENTS ARE TYPED ONCE
// Elements come from the sites' `element` and flow up. `elements` is authored
// only where a record contains an element it does not model as a part (the
// Si, O and H of a silanol, a group). build.py derives `all_elements` and
// warns where an authored element is already supplied. Symbols are validated
// against data/elements.jsonc.
//
// The claim then gets two derived sets (see bands.jsonc, references[]):
// `site_elements` from where it placed the species (its sites, the materials
// inside its specimen, and the specimen itself only where it is one
// compound), `specimen_elements` from everything it names.
//
// IDS ARE ASCII, LABELS CARRY THE UNICODE
// The key is a machine identifier (`cu_1p`), the label what a reader sees
// (`Cu⁺`). Charge is spelled out as `_0`, `_1p`, `_2m`. Element-symbol ids
// always mean the element (`co_0` is cobalt metal, never carbon monoxide).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SCHEMA
//   <key>:             ASCII identifier, referenced by
//                        band.references[].measured_on and .diluent
//     label:           display text, Unicode (required)
//     level:           site | material (required). A site is drawn filled,
//                        a material hollow.
//     refines:         the key of the record this one specialises, on the
//                        same level (optional, see above)
//     kind:            what the record is, by level (a refinement inherits it).
//                        Material: oxide | mixed oxide | halide, for a
//                        compound; a composite has none.
//                        Site: metal (a zero-valent metal atom) |
//                        atom (a zero-valent non-metal, Si⁰) | cation |
//                        anion | bronsted | hydroxyl | defect | interface
//     element:         the element of an atom-scale site
//     formula:         Unicode formula, for a compound (optional)
//     composition:     a formula for a material, only where neither the label
//                        nor the parts can say it (ZSM-5: MₙAlₙSi₉₆₋ₙO₁₉₂)
//     elements:        element symbols not supplied by parts or parent
//     parts:           keys this record is made of, same level or lower
//     note:            one or two sentences, shown on the Dataset page
//   Refinement attributes, at least one on any record that `refines`:
//     oxidation_state: integer (on a site root it is simply its charge)
//     charge:          integer, for a charged defect (Vₒ → Vₒ⁺)
//     coordination:    tetrahedral, octahedral, … (Ga³⁺ → Ga³⁺(t))
//     polymorph:       γ, α, anatase, … (Al₂O₃ → γ-Al₂O₃)
//     facet:           crystallographic termination (Fe₃O₄ → Fe₃O₄(001))
//     form:            nanoparticle, film electrode, … (Ag/Au on Si → film electrode)
//     stoichiometry:   O-deficient, … (ZnO → ZnO₁₋ₓ)
//
// KEEP IN SYNC: adding, renaming or removing a key here means updating
// data/bands.jsonc in the same change. build.py fails on an unresolved key.
// =============================================================================
{
  "surfaces": {

    // ── Sites: the atom-scale spot a molecule binds to, and the ions and atoms of everything else ──
    "ag_0": {
      "label": "Ag⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Ag",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic silver. Binds atomic hydrogen weakly and formate strongly enough, which is the combination that gives it the lowest barrier to formate of the three coinage metals."
    },
    "ag_111": {
      "label": "Ag⁰(111)",
      "level": "site",
      "refines": "ag_0",
      "facet": "(111)",
      "note": "Metallic silver exposing its (111) plane, the close-packed face of an fcc metal: a hexagonal layer in which each surface atom keeps nine of its twelve neighbours, the smoothest and lowest-energy face. It offers atop, bridge and two kinds of threefold hollow, fcc and hcp, which differ only in whether an atom of the second layer lies beneath. It is the slab a calculation reaches for when it models a silver electrode. A refinement of Ag⁰ by its facet: a search for Ag⁰ finds it."
    },
    "au_0": {
      "label": "Au⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Au",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic gold. Binds formate too weakly to accumulate it, the opposite failure to copper, which binds hydrogen too strongly."
    },
    "co_0": {
      "label": "Co⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Co",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "cu_0": {
      "label": "Cu⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Metallic copper. Carries ν(CO) roughly 40–80 cm⁻¹ below the Cu⁺ carbonyl."
    },
    "cu_110": {
      "label": "Cu⁰(110)",
      "level": "site",
      "refines": "cu_0",
      "facet": "(110)",
      "note": "Metallic copper exposing its (110) plane, the most open of the three low-index faces of an fcc metal: close-packed rows of atoms separated by troughs, each surface atom keeping seven of its twelve neighbours. CO sits atop the rows. A refinement of Cu⁰ by its facet, so a search for Cu⁰ finds it."
    },
    "pd_0": {
      "label": "Pd⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Pd",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "pd_100": {
      "label": "Pd⁰(100)",
      "level": "site",
      "refines": "pd_0",
      "facet": "(100)",
      "note": "Metallic palladium exposing its (100) plane: a square net of surface atoms, each keeping eight of its twelve neighbours, with atop, twofold bridge and fourfold hollow sites. CO prefers the bridge on this face, which is why its band lies in the bridged range across most of the coverage. A refinement of Pd⁰ by its facet, so a search for Pd⁰ finds it."
    },
    "pt_0": {
      "label": "Pt⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Pt",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "pt_100": {
      "label": "Pt⁰(100)",
      "level": "site",
      "refines": "pt_0",
      "facet": "(100)",
      "note": "Metallic platinum exposing its (100) plane: a square net of surface atoms, each keeping eight of its twelve neighbours, with atop, bridge and fourfold hollow sites. Clean, the top layer reconstructs into a denser quasi-hexagonal one; adsorbed CO lifts that back to the square (1×1) net, on which CO sits atop and bridged. A refinement of Pt⁰ by its facet, so a search for Pt⁰ finds it."
    },
    "ru_0": {
      "label": "Ru⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "metal",
      "element": "Ru",
      "oxidation_state": 0
    },
    "si_0": {
      "label": "Si⁰",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "atom",
      "element": "Si",
      "oxidation_state": 0,
      "note": "Elemental silicon, the internal-reflection prism a film electrode is deposited on."
    },
    "ag_1p": {
      "label": "Ag⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Ag",
      "oxidation_state": 1,
      "note": "Oxidised silver, carrying a carbonyl well above the gas-phase CO value because there is little back-donation to lower it. Its position moves when the silver is alloyed, which is what makes it a probe of alloying rather than only of oxidation state."
    },
    "al_3p": {
      "label": "Al³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Al",
      "oxidation_state": 3,
      "note": "An aluminium cation of alumina or of a zeolite framework. Coordinatively unsaturated, it is the Lewis acid site of γ-Al₂O₃."
    },
    "au_1p": {
      "label": "Au⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Au",
      "oxidation_state": 1,
      "note": "Oxidised gold. Distinguished from metallic Au⁰ by a carbonyl above 2120 cm⁻¹; the attribution is contested, since restructured metallic gold gives bands low enough to confuse the picture from the other side."
    },
    "ce_3p": {
      "label": "Ce³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Ce",
      "oxidation_state": 3,
      "note": "A reduced cerium cation, left beside an oxygen vacancy when ceria gives up lattice oxygen; the cerium side of a reduced metal-ceria perimeter."
    },
    "ce_4p": {
      "label": "Ce⁴⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Ce",
      "oxidation_state": 4,
      "note": "The cerium cation of stoichiometric CeO₂."
    },
    "cs_1p": {
      "label": "Cs⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cs",
      "oxidation_state": 1
    },
    "cu_1p": {
      "label": "Cu⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 1,
      "note": "The carbonyl marker that separates a partly oxidised copper surface from Cu⁰."
    },
    "cu_2p": {
      "label": "Cu²⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Cu",
      "oxidation_state": 2,
      "note": "Fully oxidised copper, carrying a carbonyl some 25 cm⁻¹ above the Cu⁺ one. Rare in this dataset, because a working methanol catalyst is reduced: where a source reports it on one, it is claiming a minority site that survived the reduction, and the claim is worth reading against the Cu⁺ attribution it sits next to."
    },
    "fe_2p": {
      "label": "Fe²⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Fe",
      "oxidation_state": 2,
      "note": "The ferrous cation of magnetite, Fe₃O₄."
    },
    "fe_3p": {
      "label": "Fe³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Fe",
      "oxidation_state": 3,
      "note": "The ferric cation of haematite and of magnetite."
    },
    "ga_3p": {
      "label": "Ga³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Ga",
      "oxidation_state": 3,
      "note": "A gallium cation of Ga₂O₃ or of a Ga-doped oxide. The Ga-H papers write Ga-H and state no charge; they are filed here because the hydride forms by heterolytic H₂ splitting on a Ga³⁺-O²⁻ pair, which is the author’s reading rather than the papers’."
    },
    "hf_4p": {
      "label": "Hf⁴⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Hf",
      "oxidation_state": 4
    },
    "in_3p": {
      "label": "In³⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "In",
      "oxidation_state": 3
    },
    "mo_4p": {
      "label": "Mo⁴⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Mo",
      "oxidation_state": 4,
      "note": "A reduced molybdenum cation of MoOₓ, as in MoO₂."
    },
    "mo_5p": {
      "label": "Mo⁵⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Mo",
      "oxidation_state": 5,
      "note": "A partly reduced molybdenum cation of MoOₓ, beside an oxygen vacancy."
    },
    "mo_6p": {
      "label": "Mo⁶⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Mo",
      "oxidation_state": 6,
      "note": "The fully oxidised molybdenum cation of MoO₃ and of the unreduced MoOₓ layer."
    },
    "mo_dp": {
      "label": "Moᵟ⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "Mo",
      "note": "Molybdenum reduced below Mo⁴⁺ with no integer charge assigned, written δ+ the way the MoOₓ literature writes it. The one cation here without an oxidation state, because δ is the statement: partly reduced, not a stated charge."
    },
    "k_1p": {
      "label": "K⁺",
      "level": "site",
      "kind": "cation",
      "element": "K",
